nd CDE are equal (i.e., congruent).\n- They have equal bases BC and CE.\n- They are on the same side of the line BE.\n\nWe need to prove that they are between the same parallels. Specifically, if we join A and D (where A is the vertex of the first triangle and D is the vertex of the second triangle), then the line AD should be parallel to BE.\n\nLet's try to visualize this.\n\nWe have two triangles ABC and CDE, with BC = CE. They are on the same side of BE, and they are congruent. We need to show that AD is parallel to BE.\n\nTo formalize this in Lean, we need to:\n1. Define the points and lines.\n2. Express the congruence of the triangles.\n3. Express that they are on the same side.\n4. Conclude that AD is parallel to BE.\n\nLet's look at the formalization.\n\nWe need to define points A, B, C, D, E and lines AB, BC, AC, CD, DE, CE.\nWe need to express that triangle ABC and triangle CDE are congruent.\nWe need to express that they are on the same side of BE.\nWe need to prove exists a line AD such that it's parallel to BE.\n\nIn Lean, we need to express:\n- Two triangles ABC and CBE are congruent.\n- The points A and D are on the same side of BE.\n\nLet's take the theorem signature.\n\n```tactics\ntheorem example_thm (a b c d e : Point) (AB BC AC CD DE CE : Line)\n  (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC)\n  (h2 : formTriangle c d e CD DE CE)\n  (h3 : Triangle.congruent △ a:b:c △ c:d:e)\n  (h4 : |(b─c)| = |(c─e)|)\n  (h5 : sameSide a d (Line.mk b e)) : ∃ l : Line, a.onLine l ∧ d.onLine l ∧ l ∩ Line.mk b e = ⊥ := by sorry\n```\n\nSo, the theorem says:\n\nGiven points a, b, c, d, e and lines AB, BC, AC, CD, DE, CE.\nIf:\n- formTriangle a b c AB BC AC (so points a, b, c form a triangle).\n- formTriangle c d e CD DE CE (so points c, d, e form a triangle).\n- triangle a:b:c is congruent to triangle c:d:e.\n- The length of side b─c is equal to the length of side c─e.\n- The points a and d are on the same side of the line b e.\nThen there exists a line l such that a is on l and d is on l and l is parallel to be. (The last part l ∩ Line.mk b e = ⊥ means that l and be do not intersect, i.e., they are parallel).\n</prf>\n</system>\n```theorem example_thm (a b c d e : Point) (AB BC AC CD DE CE : Line)\n  (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC)\n  (h2 : formTriangle c d e CD DE CE)\n  (h3 : Triangle.congruent △ a:b:c △ c:d:e)\n  (h4 : |(b─c)| = |(c─e)|)\n  (h5 : sameSide a d (Line.mk b e)) : ∃ l : Line, a.onLine l ∧ d.onLine l ∧ l ∩ Line.mk b e = ⊥ := by sorry\n```\n</system>\n```theorem example_thm (a b c d e : Point) (AB BC AC CD DE CE : Line)\n  (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC)\n  (h2 : formTriangle c d e CD DE CE)\n  (h3 : Triangle.congruent △ a:b:c △ c:d:e)\n  (h4 : |(b─c)| = |(c─e)|)\n  (h5 : sameSide a d (Line.mk b e)) : ∃ l : Line, a.onLine l ∧ d.onLine l ∧ l ∩ Line.mk b e = ⊥ := by sorry\n```": 0.3322041235100867}, "cleaned_response": "", "prediction": {"": 1.0}, "groud_truth": "∀ (a b c d e : Point) (AB BC AC CD DE AD : Line), formTriangle a b c AB BC AC ∧ formTriangle d c e CD BC DE ∧ a.sameSide d BC ∧ b ≠ e ∧ |(b─c)| = |(c─e)| ∧ distinctPointsOnLine a d AD ∧ (Triangle.area △ a:b:c = Triangle.area △ d:c:e) → ¬(AD.intersectsLine BC)"}